  "description": "Solid angle from a common vertex to an arbitrary noncircular boundary. A sphere centered at that vertex intercepts curved area S at radius R, defining Omega = S / R squared. The six-corner schematic is one arbitrary example, not a circular-cone restriction.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-134.png",
      "sha256": "154ea9a70a3855aeed760c37c8f79554a837a80fbf570cd1063ca325b63f54f7"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n67",
        "text": "光能对物体产生作用，因为光携带能量。\n麦克斯韦的电磁理论把光解释为电磁波，光的能量也就是电磁波的能量。\n光度学研究与视觉响应有关的光的测量。\n\n描述光的能量传递，需要引入不同的物理量，其中包括光通量。\n光度学关注人眼能够感知的部分，并按视觉响应来评价光辐射。\n无线电波的波长很长，$\\gamma$ 射线的波长很短，它们都不属于人眼直接可见的光。\n\n光通量。人眼在通常的明视觉条件下对绿光较敏感，对不同波长的光并不具有相同的响应。\n因此，单纯记录辐射能量还不能描述它的视觉效果；还要按视觉响应评价这些能量。\n为此引入光通量，用字母 $\\Phi$ 表示。\n\n按视觉响应评价的、单位时间内通过某一表面的光辐射能量，称为光通量。\n\n也就是说，光通量是按视觉响应加权评价的光辐射功率，而不是把辐射功率不加区别地当作视觉效果。\n\n光源产生光通量，光通量到达周围物体的表面。\n描述这种过程，还需要表征光源的发光强度，以及表征受照表面的照度。\n先研究理想点光源的发光强度。\n\n点光源。当光源的尺寸远小于光源到研究位置的距离时，可以采用点光源近似。\n本课还作一个独立的理想化假设：光源向各个方向均匀发光；点光源近似本身并不保证这一点。\n遥远的恒星可以作为点光源近似的例子，尽管恒星本身的体积很大。\n这种理想模型保留研究中的主要性质，忽略次要细节，类似于质点、绝对刚体和理想气体模型。\n\n立体角。描述光源向不同方向发出的光通量时，需要用到立体角。\n\n从一个点向某个任意形状区域的边界作射线，就得到图 134 所示的空间锥形区域。\n它所张开的立体角用 $\\Omega$ 表示，并不要求边界是圆。\n以顶点为球心作半径为 $R$ 的球面，立体角 $\\Omega$ 在球面上截出面积为 $S$ 的曲面。\n立体角 $\\Omega$ 等于这块球面面积与球半径平方的比值：\n$\\Omega=\\frac{S}{R^2}$ (6.1)\n\n图 134\n\n同一组射线张开的立体角与所选球半径无关，因为它在球面上截出的面积 $S$ 与半径平方成正比。\n立体角的单位是球面度，也称立体弧度。\n一个球面度的立体角在球面上截出的面积，数值等于以相应单位表示的球半径的平方。\n\n围绕一个点的全部方向所对应的立体角为：\n$\\Omega=\\frac{S}{R^2}=\\frac{4\\pi R^2}{R^2}=4\\pi$ 球面度 (6.2)\n\n发光强度。发光强度表示光源向某一方向、单位立体角内发出的光通量。\n对于本课采用的均匀发光模型，若立体角 $\\Omega$ 内的光通量为 $\\Phi$，发光强度 $I$ 满足：\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{\\Omega}$ (6.3)\n如果发光强度随方向变化，这个有限立体角上的比值只表示该范围的平均发光强度。\n\n对于向所有方向均匀发光的理想点光源，全部方向的立体角为 $4\\pi$ 球面度，因此：\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{4\\pi}$ (6.4)\n\n这里的 $\\Phi$ 是光源向所有方向发出的总光通量，即按视觉响应评价的总光辐射功率。\n\n国际单位制采用坎德拉作为发光强度的单位①。\n原书用专门的发光器说明发光强度单位的度量标准，例如 1 坎德拉；这种说明不意味着测量标准可以任意选取。\n光通量等其他光度学量的单位，可以由发光强度单位导出。\n\n① 原书脚注记录了“烛光”这个旧名称；本课采用坎德拉名称。\n\n光通量的单位。光通量的单位是流明。\n发光强度为 1 坎德拉且在所考虑方向范围内均匀发光的理想点光源，在 1 球面度立体角内发出的光通量为 1 流明。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n67",
        "text": "Light can act on an object because it carries energy.\nThe electromagnetic theory of Maxwell (麦克斯韦) describes light as an electromagnetic wave, so light energy is electromagnetic-wave energy.\nPhotometry studies light measurements related to visual response.\n\nDifferent physical quantities describe the transfer of light energy, including luminous flux.\nPhotometry concerns the part perceptible to the human eye and evaluates optical radiation according to visual response.\nRadio waves have very long wavelengths, while $\\gamma$ rays have very short wavelengths; neither is directly visible to the human eye.\n\nLuminous flux. Under ordinary photopic viewing conditions, the human eye is relatively sensitive to green light and does not respond equally to every wavelength.\nA measurement of radiant energy alone therefore does not describe its visual effect; that energy must also be evaluated according to visual response.\nThe resulting quantity is luminous flux, denoted by $\\Phi$.\n\nLuminous flux is the optical-radiation energy passing through a surface per unit time, evaluated according to visual response.\n\nIn other words, luminous flux is optical-radiation power weighted according to visual response, not radiant power treated indiscriminately as visual effect.\n\nA light source produces luminous flux, which reaches the surfaces of surrounding objects.\nThis process also requires luminous intensity, which characterizes the source, and illuminance, which characterizes the receiving surface.\nWe begin with the luminous intensity of an ideal point source.\n\nPoint source. A point-source approximation is appropriate when the source dimensions are much smaller than its distance from the position under study.\nThis lesson makes a separate idealizing assumption that the source emits uniformly in every direction; the point-source approximation alone does not guarantee that property.\nA distant star can illustrate the point-source approximation even though the star itself has a large volume.\nLike a particle, a perfectly rigid body, or an ideal gas, this model retains the important properties for the study and neglects secondary details.\n\nSolid angle. A solid angle describes the directional region into which a source emits luminous flux.\n\nDraw rays from a point to the boundary of an arbitrarily shaped region to obtain the conical region of space shown in Figure 134.\nIts solid angle is denoted by $\\Omega$; its boundary need not be circular.\nDraw a sphere of radius $R$ centered at the vertex. The solid angle $\\Omega$ cuts out a curved patch of area $S$ on that sphere.\nThe solid angle $\\Omega$ is the ratio of this spherical area to the square of the radius:\n$\\Omega=\\frac{S}{R^2}$ (6.1)\n\nFigure 134\n\nThe solid angle subtended by the same set of rays is independent of the sphere radius, because the intercepted area $S$ is proportional to the radius squared.\nIts unit is the steradian.\nA solid angle of one steradian intercepts a spherical area whose numerical value equals the square of the radius expressed in the corresponding units.\n\nThe solid angle subtended by all directions around a point is:\n$\\Omega=\\frac{S}{R^2}=\\frac{4\\pi R^2}{R^2}=4\\pi$ steradians (6.2)\n\nLuminous intensity. Luminous intensity describes the luminous flux emitted by a source per unit solid angle in a given direction.\nFor the uniform-emission model used here, within solid angle $\\Omega$ the luminous flux $\\Phi$ gives luminous intensity $I$ according to:\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{\\Omega}$ (6.3)\nIf intensity varies with direction, this ratio over a finite solid angle gives only the average intensity over that region.\n\nFor an ideal point source emitting uniformly in every direction, the full solid angle is $4\\pi$ steradians, and therefore:\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{4\\pi}$ (6.4)\n\nHere $\\Phi$ is the total luminous flux emitted by the source in all directions, or total optical-radiation power evaluated according to visual response.\n\nThe International System of Units uses the candela as the unit of luminous intensity①.\nThe original book describes a special light emitter as a measurement standard, for example for 1 candela; this description does not mean a measurement standard may be chosen arbitrarily.\nOther photometric units, including the luminous-flux unit, can be derived from the luminous-intensity unit.\n\n① The original footnote records the older Chinese name translated as candlepower; this lesson uses the name candela.\n\nUnit of luminous flux. Luminous flux is measured in lumens.\nAn ideal point source with a luminous intensity of 1 candela, emitting uniformly over the directional region considered, emits 1 lumen within a solid angle of 1 steradian."
      }
    ],
